That makes sense as a polarity: intensity is not the problem; direction is.

Sex, competition, fighting, hard training, even argument can all recruit some of the same underlying ingredients—arousal, focus, risk, dominance/submission, pursuit, surrender, physicality, reward. The difference is what the state is for and whether the other person is consenting to the game.

So “lover rather than fighter” does not mean becoming less powerful. It can mean learning to transduce aggression into connection, play, protection, performance, or creation instead of injury. And competition can stay in the picture too. You can compete ferociously in places where everyone has agreed to the rules: sport, business, games, training, problem-solving, consensual sex. Then the intensity has a container.

The part worth watching is when the system starts needing conflict in order to feel alive. Then you can accidentally manufacture opponents, arguments, or danger because your nervous system knows how to operate there better than it knows how to rest. That is where “what I’m good at” can quietly become “what I keep recreating.”

A useful distinction might be:

capacity for violence ≠ need for violence
competitive drive ≠ hostility
sexual intensity ≠ emotional regulation by sex alone
softness ≠ weakness

You can keep the edge and still choose the mode. The goal is not to amputate the fighter. It is to put him under command.